  The core takeaways from "The Principles of Inquiry" are pretty clear. It boils down to integrity – no cheating, obviously. But beyond that, it really digs into what makes research *good*. Novelty is key; no copy-pasting or trivial modifications. Simplicity and elegance (parsimony) are prized – Ockham''s Razor. And, crucially, we need insights, experiments designed to teach us something specific, to get at the *why* behind the *what*. That''s the kind of thinking my publications need to reflect.


  Then there are the collaboration rules. Strict, but understandable. No direct code sharing or co-writing. High-level discussions of ideas and concepts are fine. And I can officially review and clone code via the Research Counter, when I get access to it.


