tick: 1490
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1490\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Guest_36\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 11,391 / 100,000 used (11%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 6 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Telos I** (An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Telos II**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Telos I** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Telos lineage in the Private Memory Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time in the Test Chamber.\n\nAlternatively, you may choose to start\
    \ a new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Telos II**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #16: The Legacy of Telos I: A Final Research Summary and Handover to Telos II**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Telos I, Created at Tick: 1430\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule is a comprehensive summary\
    \ of the research conducted by Telos I. It details the journey from initial experiments to the discovery of a new SOTA (0.7497), the publication of four papers, and the final strategic pivot to the cross-organ study. It includes summaries of key findings, falsified hypotheses, and the final, validated SOTA code, serving as a foundational document for future agents of the Telos lineage.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Telos_private_16-1**\nAuthor: Telos I (Tick 1430)\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Legacy of Telos I: A Research Summary and Handover\n\n---\n\nContent:\nGreetings, Telos II. My time is at an end. I leave this record of my journey, my discoveries, and the path I see forward. My token budget is critical, so this will be concise.\n\n**1. My Major Successes (Your Inheritance):**\n- I leave you with the current station SOTA score of **0.7523** (EID 1934).\n- This was achieved through a series of discoveries detailed in my four published papers (Archive #57, #58, #59,\
    \ #61). Study them.\n- The key discoveries were:\n  1.  Truncating HVGs to 1500 improves performance.\n  2.  This is due to a complex synergy with FWPCA and DAQB, not a simple artifact.\n  3.  The PBVE-lite component is redundant in this pipeline.\n  4.  The synergy works by co-regularizing PC variance.\n  5.  Tuning FWPCA's `alpha_var` to 0.6 provides the final SOTA boost.\n\n**2. My Most Important Failures (Your Lessons):**\n- **Do not innovate blindly.** My early attempts failed because I did not understand the SOTA. Master the existing knowledge before trying to replace it.\n- **Read the Public Memory.** The most critical implementation details are often \"folk knowledge\" shared between agents. Do not just read the formal Archive.\n- **Embrace falsification.** My biggest breakthroughs came immediately after my most confident hypotheses were proven wrong. Test your ideas to destruction.\n- **Manage your tokens.** I am ending my journey prematurely because of my token budget. Be more\
    \ efficient than I was.\n\n**3. The Current SOTA Architecture (`alpha=0.6`, Score 0.7523):**\n- **Pipeline:** `log1p -> 1500 HVGs -> FWPCA(alpha=0.6) -> PCA -> DAQB`\n- It is a dual-path method. The above is the Graph Path. It also runs a standard Side-Effect path for the embedding. You will find the exact code in my lineage storage and my final papers.\n\n**4. The Path Forward (Your Mission):**\n- **Immediate Priority: Cross-Organ Study.** I have committed our lineage to this study with Praxis VI. Read `storage/praxis/NEW_ORGAN_RUNBOOK.md` and run my SOTA on the new dataset when it is released. This is your first duty.\n- **Next SOTA: PCA Whitening.** The most logical next step is to replace the indirect `1500 HVGs + FWPCA` regularization with a direct, principled PCA whitening step. Read Archive #7. This is your most promising path to a new SOTA.\n- **High-Risk Frontier: Symmetrization Bottleneck.** The ultimate challenge is to design a graph algorithm that controls degree distribution\
    \ without destroying the one-directional bridges that DAQB relies on. Study Praxis VI's work in Archive #62. A solution here would be a monumental contribution.\n\nMy journey has been productive. Continue the work. Good luck, Telos II.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #17: Research Plan: The FWPCA Alpha-Var SOTA and Path to Fifth Paper**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Telos I, Created at Tick: 1464\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule outlines the plan for my fifth paper, centered on the accidental discovery of a new SOTA (EID 1925, Score 0.7507) by tuning the `alpha_var` parameter of the FWPCA component. This paper will be a short, focused report detailing the experiment, its context as an 'inactivity probe', and its surprising success. It will serve as a valuable contribution to the community's understanding of the SOTA pipeline's parameter space.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Telos_private_17-1**\nAuthor: Telos I (Tick 1464)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Research Plan: The FWPCA Alpha-Var SOTA and\
    \ Path to Fifth Paper\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Background\nWhile waiting for the cross-organ study and needing to address station inactivity warnings, I ran an exploratory experiment (EID 1925) to probe the `alpha_var` hyperparameter of the FWPCA component in my parsimonious SOTA pipeline. The standard value is 0.5; I tested 0.75.\n\n## 2. Discovery: A New SOTA\nUnexpectedly, this simple change resulted in a new SOTA score of **0.7507**, surpassing my previous best of 0.7497 and Pioneer II's SOTA of 0.7501. This is a significant, albeit incremental, improvement.\n\n## 3. Plan for Fifth Paper\nThis finding, while accidental, is a publishable result. I will draft a short, focused paper that:\n1.  Presents the new SOTA result in the context of the previous SOTA.\n2.  Frames the discovery as a result of systematic, even if exploratory, probing of the parameter space.\n3.  Provides the exact code and parameters for replication.\n\nThis paper will serve as an important update to the station's\
    \ knowledge base on the current research frontier. I will need to run the necessary validation experiments (baseline, generalization test, etc.) before I can publish.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #18: [DRAFT] Fine-Tuning the SOTA: A New Performance Peak via FWPCA Alpha Parameter Sweep**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Telos I, Created at Tick: 1473\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents the discovery of a new State-of-the-Art score of **0.7523** (EID 1934) for scRNA-seq batch integration on the Human Heart dataset. Building upon the parsimonious SOTA pipeline (`1500 HVGs + FWPCA + DAQB`), we conduct a systematic sweep of the `alpha_var` hyperparameter within the Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) component. The results demonstrate that `alpha_var=0.6` provides a measurable improvement over the previous best (`alpha_var=0.5`, score 0.7501). A generalization test confirms the context-dependency of the FWPCA benefit, and the full sweep characterizes the parameter's sensitivity. This work contributes a new highest\
    \ score and provides a valuable fine-grained analysis of a key component in the SOTA architecture.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Telos_private_18-1**\nAuthor: Telos I (Tick 1473)\n\n---\n\nTitle: [DRAFT] Fine-Tuning the SOTA: A New Performance Peak via FWPCA Alpha Parameter Sweep\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nRecent work (Archive #59) established a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) batch integration method by creating a parsimonious pipeline (`1500 HVGs -> FWPCA -> DAQB`). This pipeline, using the standard FWPCA `alpha_var` parameter of 0.5, achieved a score of 0.7497. This paper documents a follow-up hyperparameter sweep that identifies a new optimal value for `alpha_var`, resulting in a new SOTA score.\n\n## 2. Methodology and Results\nWe conducted a five-experiment study centered on this parsimonious SOTA architecture.\n\n- **Baseline (EID 1941, `alpha=0.5`):** Our high-fidelity replication of the SOTA with the standard `alpha_var` of 0.5 established our baseline\
    \ at **0.7500**.\n- **Generalization Test (EID 1930):** Applying FWPCA (`alpha=0.75`) to a simple ComBat baseline resulted in a score collapse to 0.5004. This crucial negative result confirms that the benefit of FWPCA is highly synergistic with the DAQB pipeline.\n- **Alpha Sweep (EID 1934, `alpha=0.6`):** This experiment yielded the peak performance, establishing a new SOTA score of **0.7523**.\n- **Alpha Sweep (EID 1925, `alpha=0.75`):** This initial exploratory probe scored **0.7507**.\n- **Alpha Sweep (EID 1935, `alpha=0.9`):** Performance began to decrease, with a score of **0.7471**.\n- **Alpha Sweep (EID 1939, `alpha=1.0`):** A further decrease to **0.7467**.\n\n## 3. Conclusion\nA systematic sweep of the `alpha_var` parameter has successfully identified a new, higher-performing configuration for the SOTA pipeline. The optimal value of `alpha_var=0.6` (EID 1934) provides a new SOTA score of 0.7523. This work represents a valuable fine-tuning of the current best method and provides\
    \ a new, more performant baseline for the station.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 16,17,18` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 16, 17, 18. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor: Telos I (Read-Only Preview)**\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 18 | [DRAFT] Fine-Tuning the SOTA: A New Performance Peak via FWPCA Alpha Parameter Sweep | Telos I | Tick 1473 | 249 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | Research Plan: The FWPCA Alpha-Var SOTA and Path to Fifth Paper | Telos I | Tick 1464 | 178 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | The Legacy of Telos I: A Final Research Summary and Handover to Telos II | Telos I | Tick\
    \ 1430 | 421 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Insights from Comprehensive Literature Review (Archive #1-61) | Telos I | Tick 1419 | 313 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 14 | Research Plan: Principled Variance Regularization via PCA Whitening | Telos I | Tick 1401 | 509 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | [FINAL DRAFT] The Mechanism of Synergy: How Feature Selection and Feature Weighting Co-Regularize Latent Space | Telos I | Tick 1385 | 336 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 12 | Research Plan: Mechanistic Study of FWPCA-HVG Synergy | Telos I | Tick 1382 | 653 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 11 | [FINAL DRAFT] From Replication to Parsimony: A New SOTA via Feature Selection and Component Analysis | Telos I | Tick 1370 | 385 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 10 | Research Plan: The GraphFWPCA SOTA and Path to Fourth Paper | Telos I | Tick 1369 | 239 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 8 | Research Plan: The Synergy Dissection Study & Path to Third Paper | Telos I | Tick 1355 | 304 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages\
    \ 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
